B3.3 – Cost of Exceptions (2 Marks)
The time required to validate 1,000,000 bills was measured using return codes and exceptions for 0%, 1%, and 50% invalid bills.
Code
#include <iostream>
#include <chrono>
#include <stdexcept>
using namespace std;
using namespace chrono;

bool validateReturn(bool invalid) {
    return !invalid;
}

bool validateException(bool invalid) {
    if (invalid)
        throw runtime_error("Invalid bill");

    return true;
}

int main() {
    const int N = 1000000;

    int percentages[] = {0, 1, 50};

    for (int p : percentages) {
        int invalidCount = N * p / 100;

        auto start1 = high_resolution_clock::now();

        int valid1 = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            bool invalid = i < invalidCount;

            if (validateReturn(invalid))
                valid1++;
        }

        auto end1 = high_resolution_clock::now();

        auto start2 = high_resolution_clock::now();

        int valid2 = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            bool invalid = i < invalidCount;

            try {
                if (validateException(invalid))
                    valid2++;
            }
            catch (const exception&) {
                // Invalid bill handled
            }
        }

        auto end2 = high_resolution_clock::now();

        cout << "\nInvalid bills: " << p << "%\n";

        cout << "Return code time: "
             << duration_cast<milliseconds>(end1-start1).count()
             << " ms\n";

        cout << "Exception time: "
             << duration_cast<milliseconds>(end2-start2).count()
             << " ms\n";

        cout << "Valid bills: " << valid1 << " and " << valid2 << '\n';
    }
}


Results table
Invalid bills	Return-code time	Exception time
0%	___ ms	___ ms
1%	___ ms	___ ms
50%	___ ms	___ ms

Observation: Exceptions are efficient when errors are rare because normal execution does not require handling error conditions at every step. When invalid bills become frequent, throwing and catching exceptions increases execution time.

B3.4 – Engineering Trade-Off (2 Marks)
Comparison
Approach	Advantages	Disadvantages
Virtual functions	Easy to extend, no need to modify existing classes	Small virtual-call overhead
Switch statement	Simple and may be faster for small fixed types	Must modify switch for every new bill type
Exceptions	Clean error handling; suitable for rare errors	Expensive when errors occur frequently
Return codes	Fast and predictable	Requires checking error values everywhere

Conclusion
Virtual functions are suitable when new bill types are frequently added because they follow the Open-Closed Principle. Switch statements are suitable when the bill types are fixed and simple. Exceptions are best used for exceptional conditions, while return codes are more suitable when errors occur frequently.
Alternative: std::variant and std::visit
Using std::variant allows different bill types to be stored in one container, and std::visit processes the correct bill type.
- Time complexity: \(O(n)\)
- Space complexity: approximately the size of the largest bill type plus a type tag per element.
- It avoids virtual pointers and dynamic allocation.
- It is suitable when the set of bill types is known at compile time.
Final conclusion: Virtual functions provide better extensibility, switch statements provide simple implementation, and std::variant provides efficient type-safe handling of a fixed set of bill types. Exceptions should be used for rare errors rather than frequent validation failures.

